From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Victor Momplet <mompletvictor@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:01:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(energy): partial coverage, after-burn once, today's step
 estimate, no blocking on a cold cache, whole history
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

- Heart-rate route (cardio / other / intervals): seconds the strap missed count at basal, so the net is
  Keytel's active seconds above basal (`HrIntegral.grossOverWindow`); a 70 %-covered run is no longer
  charged the whole hour's basal against a 70 % gross.
- Unlogged activity leaves out the quarter hour after each window (POST_WORKOUT_EXCLUSION_S): that pulse
  is the fast after-burn the window's EPOC already counts.
- A day in progress with no step count borrows its share of the recent median (× fraction), not all of it.
- Nothing holds the mutex during reductions: the cache is a concurrent map, the lock guards its first load
  and the file write; `EnergyHooks.priced(rows) { republish }` returns within 300 ms (rows with "–" past
  it, never upstream's figure) and republishes the priced list when ready unless superseded; `install`
  warms the cache 20 s after start on a lowest-priority thread, 14 days a step, newest first.
- `series` reaches the first known day (daily rows, meals or phone days) instead of a 120-day cap: a day
  without raw streams costs a handful of empty index reads.
- A WHOOP-export workout (source "my-whoop", no raw pulse on the phone) keeps WHOOP's own kcal as an
  EXTERNAL figure; manual / live / detected rows still have upstream's Keytel figure replaced.

Load test (Robolectric, in-memory Room): a cold day of 86 400 heart-rate and 86 400 step samples reduces
in ~0.3 s, a cached one in ~20 ms.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
